<section>
    <h3>What the review changed</h3>
    <table class="compare" style="font-size: 0.55em;">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="width: 18%"></th>
                <th>First version</th>
                <th>Today</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr class="fragment">
                <td>Secrets</td>
                <td class="bad"><code>Authorization</code>, cookies, tokens written as is</td>
                <td class="good">Redacted by default, request and response</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="fragment">
                <td>Missing entry</td>
                <td class="bad">Real network call by default, silently</td>
                <td class="good">Exception, never the network</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="fragment">
                <td><code>stream()</code></td>
                <td class="bad">Broken, and <code>getContent()</code> serialised the requests</td>
                <td class="good"><code>AsyncResponse</code>, recorded chunk after chunk</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="fragment">
                <td>Options</td>
                <td class="bad"><code>timeout: 5</code> became 60 s when recording</td>
                <td class="good">Original options passed through as is</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <aside class="notes">
        <p>Concretely, here's what the review changed.</p>
        <p>[click] Secrets. In the first version, the Authorization header, cookies and tokens were written as is. Today, they're redacted by default, on the request and on the response.</p>
        <p>[click] The missing entry. Before: a real network call by default, silently. Today: an exception, and never the network.</p>
        <p>[click] stream(). In the first version, it was broken, and getContent() serialised the requests: no more concurrency. Today, the recorder relies on AsyncResponse and records chunk after chunk.</p>
        <p>[click] And the options. A timeout: 5 became 60 seconds when recording, because the default options of the decorated client were lost. Today, the original options are passed through as is.</p>
        <p>The lesson: a decorator must be transparent. It must respect stream(), concurrency, withOptions(), the default options. And each of these rows is a bug found by a reviewer who actually ran the code. Not just read the diff: ran it.</p>
    </aside>
</section>
